{
 "id": "39",
 "track": 39,
 "title": "Prepositions: con, para, de + él/ella…, mí / ti; hacer un favor; decir",
 "cards": [
  {
   "title": "con él, con ella… ('with he')",
   "body": "Con is a Latin building block in English too (consequence). After it, Spanish uses él, ella, ellos, nosotros, usted, ustedes.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "with him / her / them",
     "es": "con él / con ella / con ellos"
    },
    {
     "en": "with us / with you all",
     "es": "con nosotros / con ustedes"
    }
   ],
   "t": 0
  },
  {
   "title": "para = for; prepositions",
   "body": "Con, para and de are prepositions: small words of movement or position. Exceptions after prepositions: mí (me) and ti (informal you).",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "for him / for us",
     "es": "para él / para nosotros"
    },
    {
     "en": "for me / for you",
     "es": "para mí / para ti"
    },
    {
     "en": "from me / from you",
     "es": "de mí / de ti"
    }
   ],
   "t": 81.3
  },
  {
   "title": "es de / es para vs está con",
   "body": "'From you' or 'for you' describes the thing: ser. 'With you' is its location, a state: estar.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "Is it from you guys?",
     "es": "¿Es de ustedes?"
    },
    {
     "en": "It's for you guys.",
     "es": "Es para ustedes."
    },
    {
     "en": "It's with you guys.",
     "es": "Está con ustedes."
    }
   ],
   "t": 251.3
  },
  {
   "title": "hacer un favor",
   "body": "La hace would mean 'he makes it'. For 'to/for her', use le.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "He's doing me a favour.",
     "es": "Me hace un favor."
    },
    {
     "en": "He's doing her a favour.",
     "es": "Le hace un favor."
    }
   ],
   "t": 342.0
  },
  {
   "title": "decir = to say / to tell",
   "body": "Hablar = to speak or talk; decir = to say or tell.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I want to tell him.",
     "es": "Le quiero decir. / Quiero decirle."
    },
    {
     "en": "I want to say it.",
     "es": "Lo quiero decir."
    }
   ],
   "t": 395.3
  },
  {
   "title": "le / les replace a hidden preposition",
   "body": "Les compro = I buy for them (the natural way). Compro para ellos is possible too.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I buy them (the things).",
     "es": "Los compro."
    },
    {
     "en": "I buy for them.",
     "es": "Les compro."
    },
    {
     "en": "They buy them for me.",
     "es": "Me los compran."
    }
   ],
   "t": 447.9
  },
  {
   "title": "Shed your prepositions",
   "body": "Me lo compra can mean 'for me' or 'from me'; context decides. Spanish uses fewer prepositions than English; listen and you'll start dropping them.",
   "t": 589.2
  }
 ],
